# Murfulu behind one door, with automatic HTTPS.
#
# Domain and credentials come from the environment, set by
# docker-compose.prod.yml. See docs/DEPLOY.md.
#
# **Why a password rather than the app's own sign-in.** Murfulu authenticates
# with `Authorization: Bearer <token>` headers, not with a session cookie, so
# a browser asking for `/` carries nothing to check — there is no request for
# `forward_auth` to forward. Gating at the edge is the only way to keep the
# corpus off the open internet without rewriting the app's auth.
#
# **Two credentials, and that is the cost.** A reader needs the site password
# to see anything, and an expert token to answer a question. The wall says who
# may look; the token still says who may rule. Nothing about the app's own
# roles changes — see docs/REVIEW.md.
#
# **What is behind it, and it is no longer an open question.** 19.5 million
# lines of Ganjoor poetry and karbon's Dehkhoda-derived lexicon. Both were open
# when this file was written and both are now settled — and they settled in
# opposite directions. This project's own licence is **Apache-2.0** since
# 2026-08-17, and it deliberately does not reach either of them: Ganjoor, asked
# whether their data are free of copyright, answer «لزوماً خیر!» — no legal
# review, most of it typed from published books whose status was never
# established, and free access is not a licence.
#
# **So `basic_auth` is not waiting on anything.** Taking it out is still the one
# edit that would publish all three, and the first of them is now known to be
# unpublishable rather than merely unchecked.
#
# **The address carries the scheme on purpose.** On a server Caddy holds the
# certificate and this is the bare domain, which turns on automatic HTTPS.
# Behind a Cloudflare Tunnel — how the sibling apps on this domain are
# published — Cloudflare terminates TLS and forwards plain HTTP, so the address
# is written `http://…` and Caddy serves port 80 without asking Let's Encrypt
# for a certificate it could never validate. **The wall is the same either
# way**: what changes is who holds the key, never whether there is a door.

{$MURFULU_SITE_ADDRESS} {
	encode gzip

	basic_auth {
		{$MURFULU_SITE_USER} {$MURFULU_SITE_PASSWORD_HASH}
	}

	# The web container serves the app and proxies /api to the API itself, so
	# this is the whole of it — see web/nginx.conf.
	reverse_proxy web:80

	log {
		# Off by default. The corpus is a research asset, and who searched for
		# which word is not something this project needs to keep.
		output discard
	}
}
